 @MrBaltonic  if it's hardware, it would only be the lack of logic to shut off unused modules. Because Intel is not bad at all at idle on CPUs.
For the high load scenario, it's clearly software optimisation as the results are inconsistent and even drop more than a tier below.
Intel knows how to make non gaming iGPUs, they just lack the gaming part, which is for the most part driver optimization. And it shows when their driver revisions finally allow the Arc cards to show their full potential. From past experience in low spec gaming, the more performant Intel igpu of my previous Skylake system was more buggy and slow than a potato GT610 (framedrops, artifacting, missing textures, etc.).

 @TitoBridge1984  "I think"? Lmfao

Hardware Unboxed literally released a video comparing the 4080 and 7900XTX 6 days ago

The 7900XTX lacks behind 7% overall when it comes to RT. It's a whopping 40% in Cyberpunk though and that shouldn't be surprising because the RT-tech in that game was codeveloped by nvidia...

 @lilpain1997  Please. I didn't move any goal posts. Fine wine doesn't come down to just drivers, even though I said it starts with them. I thought that much was understood. It's a culmination of hardware and software that results in spectacles like the 6700XT being able to sometimes match and even surpass the 3070 in recent AAA releases because Nvidia didn't give the poor card enough VRAM, despite the 6700XT costing substantially less. Or the Vega 64 squeezing ahead of the 1080Ti these days because it seems to have enjoyed longer game optimization support from AMD compared to the 1080Ti with Nvidia. That's just fact, and this leads me to the rest of your argument:

Every GPU manufacturer releases fixes and performance improvements via driver updates. Otherwise I'd be pretty disappointed with Nvidia for saddling me with sub-optimal performance in Starfield with their initial driver release for that game. You do also realize plenty of people hang onto GPUs for the better part of a decade or buy older cards, right? Maybe? Maybe not. Take a look at the Steam hardware survey when you get the chance. You're implying that you expect performance for these cards to remain stagnant for the duration of their lives since "fixing things and making the cards run as they should have later down the line is not a good thing." Yeah, I'm twisting your words a little here, but it's almost as if you're willingly disregarding the fact that new games require new optimizations from all GPU makers. AMD just seems willing to support their products for longer than Nvidia does; see the Vega 64 VS 1080 Ti example above.

Anyways, I won't keep triggering you. Said all I care to on this subject.

 @PadaV4  so Nvidia driver should be fine milk ? 
even on game that run decently at launch Intel find a way to improve. 

The potential is there and for a first run at it, they did well. now it up to battlemage to confirm. But Intel made the impossible : make people look forward for their next gen line up.

 @thepunic5261  you have said "the E cores are beasts in gaming"

The games that work on Intel are games that ignore E cores and run exclusively on P cores. The games that have problem with E cores are games that have a problem with ignoring E cores. You can often also getter better FPS if E cores are disabled because then the P cores have more power for better boosting.

That's why your praise of E cores for gaming makes no sense.

 @em.dot.2  RX 6600 here, I have tested frame gen on Cyberpunk, Starfield, Dota 2, Apex Legends and WoW Wotlk Classic. In all those games frame gen approx doubles fps +- (depending on game).

Frame gen driver is still "preview driver" so it's not yet officially released and for some games it works better (Starfield) but in general with all listed titles frame gen on this gpu is more than worth it.
Everything feels more smoother even in preview driver.
